-- 130: SUPERGUN reconciliation after N1b, N1c and the server-architecture evaluation. Follows 117/119/120/122/124.
-- Source doc: mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-NETCODE.md s11 (appended 2026-09-04).
-- Method step 4. Lane numbering: SUPERGUN NETCODE takes EVEN numbers from 120 up.
-- NO DESIGN PARAMETER IN s9.8 CHANGES. This seed records: two gates closing, the supersession of this lane own
-- reconstructed cost numbers by a direct measurement, an architecture ruled out by arithmetic, a judgement on the
-- witness proposal, a new deployment requirement, and two new UNKNOWNs that nobody had named.
-- Apply with tools/re_kb/apply_seed.py after the README export backup
-- (re_kb_data/_exports/re_kb_20260904-163632_pre130.surql). Idempotent and order-independent. No apostrophes in literals.
-- Do NOT re-run 07_dedup_edges.surql afterwards.
USE NS re DB kb;

UPSERT source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET kind='analysis', strength='reproduction', ref='mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-NETCODE.md s11; inputs docs/RECEIPT-RUNNER-GATE-N1.md s1.2/s8/s9, docs/RECEIPT-RUNNER-GATE4.md sA3, docs/SUPERGUN-SERVER-ARCH.md s0/s2.4/s3/s9/s11', note='Consumes three other lane results and reconciles them against the SUPERGUN design. Contains no new measurement of its own; its contributions are the supersession bookkeeping, the sim-only-budget condition on the speculation cap, the scoping of the 96 ms tenancy figure, and the witness judgement. PROVENANCE WARNING recorded in the doc: GATE-N1 s8 reports N1c on two stages while GATE4 sA3 reports four; sA3 is newer and is the result of record.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_n1b_supersedes_reconstruction SET status='confirmed', text='N1b CLOSED, and this lane own reconstructed numbers are superseded along with the column it corrected. The ENGINE lane withdrew the eligibility-weighted amortised column (seed 122 correction accepted) and re-emitted per-EVENT costs with p99 and max, per stage, with tick counts, at 300 ticks with heap_wraps 0. Stage 9 event p50/p99/max: depth 8 = 0.375/0.863/0.989, depth 30 = 1.312/2.695/3.152, depth 60 = 2.557/5.304/5.850, depth 120 = 5.191/9.207/9.463. Worst FRAME (event plus that frame straight-line tick): depth 30 = 3.220 stage 9 and 2.022 Carnival, depth 60 = 5.926 and 3.857, depth 120 = 9.511 and 7.463. ZERO events exceeded the 16.667 ms budget at any depth on either stage. TWO BOOKKEEPING POINTS. (1) The seed-122 tail extrapolation was SOUND: it projected p99 at 2.2 to 4.0 times p50 from this lane tick ratios, predicting 0.83 to 1.50 ms at depth 8, and the measured p99 is 0.863. (2) The seed-122 per-event figures are WITHDRAWN: depth 8 was reconstructed as 0.255 ms and directly measures 0.375 ms, 47 percent higher, the gap explained by run length (300 versus 200 ticks) and heap sizing. A reconstruction corrects a presentation error; it is not a substitute for a measurement, and the measurement wins. Only the ARGUMENT of the seed-122 correction survives, not its numbers.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_heap_wraps_instrumentation_rule SET status='confirmed', text='Instrumentation rule carried from the N1b re-emission. Its first emission showed 1 event over budget at depth 120 (max 18.31 ms) and an 11.78 ms outlier at depth 60; both were the runner OWN bump heap WRAPPING, because a resim consumes it depth+1 times faster and a wrap memsets up to 64 MiB inside one tick. summary.json records heap_wraps, which read exactly 1 at those two depths and 0 everywhere else, so the outliers were instrumentation and not the engine. --heap-mb now sizes the heap and both driver scripts pass 1024. ANY FUTURE TIMING RUN MUST CHECK heap_wraps BEFORE ITS NUMBERS ARE QUOTED. This is the same class as the SUPERGUN lane own rule that N failed on a seed replay is a RELATE already-exists artefact rather than a failure: a tool that reports a number without reporting its own health invites a confident wrong conclusion.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate_n1c_needed SET status='resolved', text='GATE N1c CLOSED SO FAR. The three-arm check this lane designed (arm A blk+counter, arm B plus the proven 8 bytes, arm C plus the shipped 64 KB window ctx[0x1F0000..0x200000), depth 8, 300 ticks, rollback after every eligible tick) has been run on FOUR stages -- 9, 3 Carnival, 7 Ice River, 16 River Raft (docs/RECEIPT-RUNNER-GATE4.md sA3; note that GATE-N1 s8 reports only the first two and is the older document). ARM C PASSES ON ALL FOUR. ARM B NEVER FAILED, so the escalate-to-ladder branch has still not been reached. Arm A splits FAIL on stage 9 and PASS on the other three, which is the stage-dependence result. The SUPERGUN save set survives. TWO CAVEATS CARRIED RATHER THAN DROPPED: the two river stages ran on IDLE inputs (no fighter action) because no tape matched those boots, so they exercise prop and water animation and the whole render path but not combat; and four stages is not seventeen. Arm C is UNBROKEN, not proven. The falsification condition is unchanged and still live: one arm-C failure on any future stage kills the save set and deep speculation in its current form, so the check runs on every new stage.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_speculation_cap_specified SET status='confirmed', text='SUPERGUN v1 speculation cap: 64 frames allocated hard cap, 32 frames default ACTIVE, adaptive as clamp(ceil(owd_p99) + jitter_margin, 4, 32), ring rotated modulo active_cap+1. UPGRADED from inferred to CONFIRMED FOR THE SIMULATION-COST CLAIM by N1b across four stages with p99 and max and zero events over the 16.667 ms budget at any depth, measured under a rollback-on-every-frame regime far harsher than GGPO would produce. Worst FRAME at depth 30 is 3.220 ms stage 9 and 2.022 ms Carnival (19.3 and 12.1 percent of budget); at depth 60, 5.926 and 3.857 ms; at depth 120, 9.511 and 7.463 ms. THE CAP DOES NOT MOVE ABOVE 64 DESPITE THE HEADROOM, FOR A REASON NOBODY HAD STATED: every one of these numbers is SIM-ONLY, measured in a HEADLESS runner that does not render, and quoted as a percentage of the WHOLE frame. A real client must also render, present and sample input inside the same 16.667 ms. The budget actually available for rollback is 16.667 minus render minus present minus input, and the render term is UNKNOWN (see finding:supergun_render_budget_unknown). At depth 60 the worst frame leaves 10.7 ms for everything else, which is plausible; at depth 120 it leaves 7.2 ms, which probably is not. THE HARD CAP OF 64 IS ADMISSIBLE IF AND ONLY IF render plus present plus input is at most about 10.7 ms. The cap is therefore CONFIRMED as a simulation-cost claim and NOT confirmed as a whole-frame budget claim.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_render_budget_unknown SET status='open', text='NEW, U-N10. Every SUPERGUN rollback cost number -- N1, N1b, N1c, and this lane own bench -- is SIM-ONLY: measured in the headless receipt runner, which does not render, present, or sample input, yet quoted as a percentage of the whole 16.667 ms frame. The budget actually available for rollback on a real client is 16.667 minus render minus present minus input sampling, and the render term is the long-standing U4 (present and swapchain queue depth) which is UNKNOWN. Consequence: 57 percent of budget at depth 120 means 57 percent consumed by SIMULATION ALONE, leaving 7.2 ms for everything else. This gates the hard cap of 64 (admissible only if the remainder is at most about 10.7 ms) and it means no SUPERGUN cost figure may be quoted as a whole-frame figure until it is measured. Settled only by an instrumented client that timestamps render and present alongside the tick.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_server_authority_ruled_out SET status='confirmed', text='ARCHITECTURE D (central server-authoritative netcode) IS RULED OUT FOR DEFAULT PLAY, ON LATENCY, BY ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN MEASUREMENT. The SUPERGUN architecture E races direct and relay every packet and takes the first arrival, so its latency is min(direct, relay); a central authority must route every input through itself, so its latency is relay, always. min(direct, relay) <= relay is not an empirical claim, so NO OUTCOME OF GATE N3 OR N4 CAN REVERSE IT. Supporting propagation geometry (floors, not measurements): an ON-PATH relay costs plus 0.3 to 1.7 percent (LA via Chicago or Denver to NY), but a SAME-REGION pair routed through an off-path authority pays a 78.7 ms propagation floor against roughly 0 ms direct. Server authority is structurally behind the peer design and cannot be rescued by a better transport. CONSEQUENCE FOR GATE N4: it is no longer an architecture discriminator, because it measures the same quantity for both designs; it remains the sole gate for the separate claim that OUR transport beats STEAM actual transport. CONSEQUENCE FOR THE s0 FRAMING: unchanged in substance -- the big win is still not a networking win -- but the architecture question no longer waits on a measurement.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_witness_judgement SET status='confirmed', text='JUDGEMENT REQUESTED AND GIVEN: build the WITNESS, and build it OUTSIDE the SUPERGUN netcode design. It is its own workstream consuming SUPERGUN outputs, not a part of docs/SUPERGUN-NETCODE.md s9. The two results fit together cleanly: arithmetic killed authority-as-latency (see finding:supergun_server_authority_ruled_out) and the witness delivers authority-as-trust at zero latency cost, because it sits off the critical path. THREE REASONS IT DOES NOT BELONG IN s9: it changes no parameter in s9.8 (not the save set, cap, cadence or prediction), so a design section whose parameters it cannot move is the wrong home; it is gated by different things (tape fidelity, receipt signing, server density, blk_delta encode cost U-S4), and folding it in would couple the netcode gates to a product feature gates, exactly the coupling the server-arch evaluation argues against for the two-mode design; and it reuses machinery already gated elsewhere (Gate 2: runner tape equals live tape over 301 frames with 0 unexplained differences), so re-homing it would reopen settled ground. TWO LIMITS TO STATE BEFORE ANYONE SELLS IT: the witness INHERITS the SUPERGUN open risks, so if arm C of N1c ever fails on a later stage then byte-exact witnessing breaks in the same instant the netcode does and it is not an independent check of the save set; and it detects an INCONSISTENT lie and never a CONSISTENT one, so a macro producing frame-perfect but humanly impossible inputs is accepted by both peers and therefore by the witness. Server authority does not solve that either, since the authority would still have to judge whether a pad sequence is human; that is an input-plausibility problem orthogonal to where authority lives, and neither design may be credited with solving it.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_witness_interface_obligations SET status='confirmed', text='THREE OBLIGATIONS the witness places on SUPERGUN, which therefore DO belong in s9. W1: the desync hash is a PUBLISHED CONTRACT, not an implementation detail -- FNV-1a over the save set (blk + GGPO counter + ctx[0x1F0000..0x200000)), computed every frame locally and exchanged every 30 -- because the witness compares against exactly this and a silent change to cadence or coverage silently invalidates every receipt already issued. W2: THE RECEIPT CLAIMS SIMULATION FIDELITY AND NEVER VISUAL FIDELITY. Per GATE-N1 s9 and the seed-124 pinning, a save-set hash proves sim agreement only; the geometry actually submitted lands in the host staging buffer at *(game_state+0x208) that nothing dumps, so two peers can hash-match every frame and render differently. A phrase like provably the same match is a SIM-exactness claim and must be worded so. This is the single largest way the witness could overclaim and it is the project known offline-geometry-standing-in-for-live-pixels trap wearing a product label. W3: the side-stream must not contend with the input path -- separate socket, separate pacing, and it may never delay an input send. At 33 to 72 KB/s that is about 50 to 120 packets per second and, at this lane measured 0.022 ms per sendto, about 2.6 ms of CPU per second, so the risk is PACING COUPLING and not CPU; s9.8 forbids coalescing on the input path and the side-stream must not reintroduce it by sharing a queue.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_pin_and_isolate SET status='confirmed', text='DEPLOYMENT REQUIREMENT ADDED TO THE DESIGN: any host that ticks a match -- client, relay, witness or farm -- pins its tick thread to a dedicated core and raises its priority. Measured by sg_tenancy.py running K concurrent real rr_runner processes, 9,000 ticks each on real inputs: p50 goes 0.0430 at K=1 to 0.0591 at K=32, only 37 percent, and then flattens; but MAX goes 0.415 ms at K=1 to 6.24 at K=8 to 96.0 ms at K=32. A 96 ms stall is larger than the 66.7 ms of frame delay this whole workstream exists to delete. SCOPE IT CORRECTLY, BECAUSE THE HEADLINE INVITES AN OVER-READ: this is a SERVER-DENSITY measurement of 32 unpinned unprioritised processes contending on one desktop, and at K=1 the max is 0.415 ms, so 96 ms is a contention artefact of unpinned multi-tenancy and NOT a demonstrated property of a single client. On the client this composes with the s9.8 spin-to-deadline input sampling, which already requires a core it does not share, so pinning and spinning reinforce rather than compete. Kernel bypass (AF_XDP or DPDK) is worth about 0.03 ms and is Linux-only, so it can never apply to the Windows clients: server side only, and only after N3 and N4.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_client_scheduler_exposure_unknown SET status='open', text='NEW, U-N11. The 96 ms scheduler stall is a K=32 server-density artefact and at K=1 the max is 0.415 ms, so nothing measured so far shows a single client is exposed. BUT THE CLIENT CASE IS UNMEASURED AND IT IS NOT OBVIOUSLY SAFE: a Windows client running alongside OBS, Discord and a browser is also unpinned and contended, which is the normal condition of the target population rather than an edge case. If a contended client can be preempted for tens of milliseconds then no speculation cap protects it, because the stall is outside the frame function entirely. Settled by running the tick under realistic desktop load with and without core pinning and priority elevation, reporting the max, not the p50.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_combat_writeset_changes_nothing SET status='confirmed', text='For the record, and explicitly recorded to PREVENT a plausible mistake. The real combat write set is about 1 KB per frame: p50 363 B changed, 814 B XOR plus zlib, max 3.2 KB; the earlier 120 B idle figure was an underestimate by 3 to 6 times rather than by orders of magnitude, and neither run contains a SUPER so the ceiling is about 6 to 8 KB per frame INFERRED (U-S2). THIS CHANGES NOTHING IN THE SUPERGUN DESIGN, because SUPERGUN streams INPUTS -- 4 B per frame per seat with a 64-frame redundant window -- and not STATE. The write set is the size of a STATE stream, which matters for the witness and for spectating and not at all for the netcode. Nobody should optimise the s9.8 input stream into a state stream on the strength of this number: state streaming loses on latency and correction quality, not on bandwidth. The number does usefully bound the witness side-stream at 33 to 72 KB/s.';

-- edges
RELATE finding:supergun_n1b_supersedes_reconstruction->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_n1b_supersedes_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_heap_wraps_instrumentation_rule->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_heap_wraps_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_render_budget_unknown->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_render_budget_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_server_authority_ruled_out->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_authority_ruled_out_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_witness_judgement->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_witness_judgement_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_witness_interface_obligations->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_witness_obligations_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_pin_and_isolate->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_pin_and_isolate_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_client_scheduler_exposure_unknown->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_client_sched_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_combat_writeset_changes_nothing->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_combat_writeset_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_speculation_cap_specified->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_cap_src130;
RELATE finding:supergun_gate_n1c_needed->cites->source:supergun_reconciliation_20260904 SET id=cites:sg_n1c_src130;

-- N1b is closed; annotate the request rather than deleting it, so the ask and its answer stay linked
UPDATE finding:supergun_gate_n1b_needed SET status='resolved', note='RESOLVED 2026-09-04 by the ENGINE lane N1b re-emission; see finding:supergun_n1b_supersedes_reconstruction. The amortised column was withdrawn, per-event p99 and max were emitted per stage with tick counts, and 0 events exceeded the 16.667 ms budget at any depth. The tail extrapolation that motivated this gate was validated (predicted p99 0.83 to 1.50 ms at depth 8, measured 0.863), and the seed-122 reconstructed per-event figures are withdrawn in favour of the direct measurement.';

-- the seed-122 cost table is superseded by the direct per-event measurement
UPDATE finding:supergun_gate_n1_amortisation_correction SET note='PARTLY SUPERSEDED 2026-09-04. The ARGUMENT stands and the ENGINE lane acted on it: the eligibility-weighted amortised column is withdrawn and replaced by per-EVENT costs with p99 and max. The NUMBERS in this finding are withdrawn: the depth-8 per-event cost reconstructed here as 0.255 ms measures 0.375 ms directly (300 ticks, heap_wraps 0). A reconstruction corrects a presentation error; it is not a substitute for a measurement. See finding:supergun_n1b_supersedes_reconstruction.';

-- Gate 4 cost figures are now superseded by the per-stage per-event table
UPDATE finding:supergun_gate4_cost_not_yet_quotable SET status='resolved', note='RESOLVED 2026-09-04: the N1b re-emission supplies exactly what this item asked for -- per-EVENT save, restore and resim with p99 and max, per stage, with tick counts -- so the Carnival figures no longer need reconstructing from an eligibility-weighted column. Carnival worst FRAME: 0.958 ms at depth 8, 2.022 at depth 30, 3.857 at depth 60, 7.463 at depth 120; 0 events over budget. The per-stage spread runs in both directions as this item predicted (at depth 120 stage 9 is 27 percent worse; at depth 30 Carnival is 37 percent cheaper).';

-- DEDUPLICATION: seeds 128 (research lane) and 130 (netcode lane) each recorded the same two conclusions from the
-- same underlying measurements. Two nodes citing one measurement read as two independent confirmations, which is a
-- false corroboration and exactly what this graph exists to prevent. Cross-link them explicitly rather than leaving
-- them to look independent. The 128 nodes are the ORIGINALS; the 130 nodes are this lane consumption of them.
UPDATE finding:supergun_server_authority_ruled_out SET note='NOT AN INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION. This is the SUPERGUN NETCODE lane consumption of finding:supergun_server_authority_loses_on_latency (seed 128, research lane), which is the original and rests on the same arithmetic and the same propagation geometry. Cite ONE of the two, not both, as evidence. What this node adds beyond the original is only the consequence for this lane documents: architecture D is struck from SUPERGUN-NETCODE s3, and Gate N4 is reclassified from a potential architecture discriminator to a measure of absolute transport quality.';
UPDATE finding:supergun_pin_and_isolate SET note='NOT AN INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION. This is the SUPERGUN NETCODE lane consumption of finding:supergun_pin_and_isolate_required (seed 128, research lane) and of the single tenancy measurement behind it, finding:supergun_server_tenancy_measured. Cite ONE, not both. What this node adds beyond the original is the SCOPING that the 96 ms figure is a K=32 server-density artefact rather than a client property (at K=1 the max is 0.415 ms), the resulting new open item finding:supergun_client_scheduler_exposure_unknown, and the observation that pinning composes with the s9.8 spin-to-deadline input sampling rather than competing with it.';
RELATE finding:supergun_server_authority_ruled_out->cites->finding:supergun_server_authority_loses_on_latency SET id=cites:sg_authority_dedup;
RELATE finding:supergun_pin_and_isolate->cites->finding:supergun_pin_and_isolate_required SET id=cites:sg_pin_dedup;
